`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'summary-element', kind: 'exists', selector: 'footer #summary' },
				{ id: 'my-code-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: 'script#my-code' },
				{ id: 'is-module', kind: 'attr', selector: '#my-code', name: 'type', matches: '^module$' },
				{ id: 'items-array', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '(?:const|let)\\s+items\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*\\{', when: { brief: 'items[].title' } },
				{ id: 'items-own-titles', kind: 'field', selector: '#my-code', field: 'items[].title', min: 2, when: { brief: 'items[].title' } },
				{ id: 'no-var', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '^(?![\\s\\S]*\\bvar\\s+[\\w$])[\\s\\S]*\\S' },
				{ id: 'uses-map', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '\\.map\\(' },
				{ id: 'uses-filter', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '\\.filter\\(' },
				{ id: 'uses-length', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '\\.length\\b' },
				{ id: 'no-mutation', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '^(?![\\s\\S]*\\.(?:push|pop|shift|unshift|splice|sort|reverse)\\s*\\()[\\s\\S]*\\S' },
				{ id: 'three-functions', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*titlesOf)(?=[\\s\\S]*withPrice)(?=[\\s\\S]*countOf)' },
				{ id: 'console-log', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: 'console\\.log\\(' },
				{ id: 'writes-summary', kind: 'text', selector: '#my-code', matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*[\'"]#?summary[\'"])(?=[\\s\\S]*textContent)' }
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each value with its type',
				pairs: [
					['42', 'number'],
					['“Tablecloth”', 'string'],
					['true', 'boolean: yes or no'],
					['undefined', 'there is no value: the variable was never filled'],
					['[1, 2, 3]', 'array: a numbered list']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What does the expression “5” + 3 give, where “5” is a string?',
				options: ['The string 53: plus glued the text together', 'The number 8', 'An error'],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. If one of the operands is a string, plus glues. But a string 5 minus 3 gives the number 2: minus has no gluing meaning. Data from form fields always arrives as strings, so surprises like this happen on real sites.',
					'No: JavaScript will not turn a string into a number when one operand is already a string.',
					'No: there is no error, the language silently glues the values. That silence is exactly what makes it dangerous.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What happens with const price = 100; price = 200;?',
				options: [
					'An error: a const cannot be assigned again',
					'The price becomes 200',
					'The price stays 100 and there is no error'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. const ties the name to the value for good. If a value has to change, use let; the default is const, so you don’t overwrite something by accident.',
					'No: that is what let is for. With const the script stops with a TypeError.',
					'No: JavaScript will not quietly ignore the assignment. It reports an error.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'Which array methods return a new array and leave the original unchanged?',
				options: ['map', 'filter', 'push', 'sort'],
				answer: [0, 1],
				why: [
